"A second tradition accepts Glaucons account of justice as a bargain and denies that the ring exists. Hobbes imagines a Foole who says in his heart that there is no such thing as justice and that breaking an agreement is reasonable whenever it pays. Hobbes replies that it never reliably pays. A man who breaks faith must count on not being found out, and no one can count on that. He lives by the errors of others, and if they stop erring he is cast out of the society that keeps him alive. Modern research on cooperation says something similar in the language of games: where people deal with one another repeatedly, a reputation for honesty is the most valuable thing one can own.": "7636d8e91ed9",
"And virtue is the health and beauty and well-being of the soul, and vice is the disease and weakness and deformity of the soul. Again the old question returns upon us: Is justice or injustice the more profitable? The question has become ridiculous.": "781a544a5507",
"Critics have long asked whether this answers the question that was put. Glaucon asked about justice in the ordinary sense, not stealing, not killing. Socrates replies about an inner harmony. It is not obvious that a man with a well-ordered soul would keep his hands off the market stalls, or that a thief could not be calm and self-controlled. The bridge between the two has to be built, and Plato builds it mostly by assertion.": "5439c588adf2",
"Early in the second century CE the official Yang Zhen was traveling to a new post and stopped for the night at Changyi. The magistrate of the town owed his career to him. After dark the man came to his lodging with ten catties of gold in his robe. Yang Zhen said, Your old friend knows you. How is it that you do not know your old friend? The magistrate answered, It is late at night. No one will know. Yang Zhen said, Heaven knows, the spirits know, I know, and you know. How can you say that no one knows? The magistrate went out ashamed. The story has been told to Chinese schoolchildren ever since. It contains an argument, and the magistrates half of it deserves a hearing. Of the four witnesses named, the first two cannot be produced, and the last two are the only people who stand to gain. What reason is left?": "b700dc1fcfbc",
"He then sharpens the test.": "ad1bd76390ff",
"If you hold back, say why.": "9e6b303e0533",
"Kant would say that every answer so far has made the same error. Each tries to show that justice pays, in health of soul or safety or peace of mind. But a person who is honest because honesty pays is the prudent shopkeeper of the last lesson, whose acts accord with duty and have no moral worth. To ask What do I get for being good? is already to have left the moral point of view. The reason to give back the gold is that it is not yours. The Oxford philosopher H. A. Prichard argued in 1912 that moral philosophy had rested on this mistake since Plato: it kept trying to prove that we ought to do our duty by appeal to something else, when the obligation is seen directly or not at all.": "8b1685db71ad",
"No man would keep his hands off what was not his own.": "9839101c3120",
"No man would stand fast in justice.": "a95d05e32858",
"Now suppose there were two such rings, Glaucon says, and a just man wore one and an unjust man the other. No man can be imagined to be of such an iron nature that he would stand fast in justice. No man would keep his hands off what was not his own when he could safely take what he liked out of the market. The two would end by behaving alike, and this shows that no one is just willingly. He then sharpens the test. Give the unjust man a spotless reputation along with his crimes, and give the just man the reputation of a villain, so that he is whipped and tortured for his goodness. Now say which of the two is better off. To answer Glaucon, one must show that justice is worth having for itself, with every reward removed.": "e2f007bee45e",
"On the ring itself the texts are blunt. Theres no privacy in the world for someone who does bad deeds. Youll know for yourself whether youve lied or told the truth. That is Yang Zhens third witness. The teaching of karma then adds a claim that neither Plato nor the Confucians make in this form. An intentional act leaves a trace in the mind of the one who does it and bears fruit there, and this happens by a natural process that needs no observer and no judge. Not in midair, nor mid-ocean, nor hiding in a mountain cleft is there a place to escape ones deeds. Glaucons experiment assumes that an act unseen is an act without consequences for the agent. The Buddhas reply is that the agent is where the consequences fall (Dharma and the West, D17).": "8ef1d6e3c455",
"Plato put the same challenge at the start of the Republic, and put it into the mouth of his own brother. Glaucon says that he does not believe what he is about to argue and wants to hear it refuted. Justice, on the common view, is a compromise. Everyone would like to wrong others freely and suffer no wrong in return. Since that is impossible, people agree to restrain themselves, and they keep the agreement because they are watched. Take away the watching and you will see what they are. A shepherd named Gyges, the story goes, found a gold ring on a corpse in a chasm opened by an earthquake. Sitting among the other shepherds, he happened to turn the setting of the ring inward and found that he had become invisible. He had himself sent to court, seduced the queen, killed the king with her help, and took the kingdom.": "a474fc47544b",
"Suppose now that there were two such magic rings, and the just put on one of them and the unjust the other; no man can be imagined to be of such an iron nature that he would stand fast in justice. No man would keep his hands off what was not his own when he could safely take what he liked out of the market, or go into houses and lie with any one at his pleasure, or kill or release from prison whom he would, and in all respects be like a God among men. Then the actions of the just would be as the actions of the unjust; they would both come at last to the same point.": "5a81e7c73495",
"That leaves the reader with a choice among kinds of reason. One may hold with Hobbes that the ring is a fiction and honesty is the best policy. One may hold with Plato and the Confucians that injustice damages the person who does it, whether or not anyone finds out. Or one may hold with Kant that the request for a further reason is a confusion. The question is less remote than it was. A great deal of modern life is conducted under conditions that Glaucon would have recognized: anonymous, at a distance, with little chance of being held to account. How people behave there is the old experiment, run every day on a very large scale.": "3705a4436d7c",
"The Buddha names two qualities that he calls the guardians of the world: These two bright things protect the world. What two? Conscience and prudence. The Pali words are hiri and ottappa. Hiri is an inner sense of shame, the recoil of a person who respects himself from an act beneath him. Ottappa is a regard for consequences and for the judgment of the wise. Without the two, he says, people would live like animals. They correspond closely to two of the reasons in the exercise above, the reason from character and the reason from prudence, and the tradition holds that a sound moral life needs both.": "166099eee455",
"The Confucian classics take up the case of the unobserved person directly, and their answer begins by denying that he is unobserved. The Doctrine of the Mean says: Nothing is more visible than what is hidden, and nothing more manifest than what is minute. Therefore the gentleman is watchful over himself when he is alone. The Great Learning explains the paradox. The petty man, when he is by himself, stops at nothing. When he sees a gentleman he tries to cover it up, and it does him no good, because others see him as if they saw his lungs and liver. What is real within takes shape without. And it begins with a rule against lying to oneself: one should hate what is bad as one hates a foul smell.": "750a0d7cca95",
"The answer takes Plato the remaining nine books. Its core is a comparison. A soul has parts: reason, spirit, and the appetites. It is in good order when reason governs with the support of spirit, and each part does its own work. That order is what justice in a person is, and injustice is a civil war in which the appetites have seized control. Virtue is the health and beauty and well-being of the soul, and vice is the disease and weakness and deformity of the soul. Nobody asks why he should want to be healthy if no one is watching. Once justice is seen to be the health of the thing we live by, Socrates says, the question whether it pays has become ridiculous. Gyges with his ring is a man free to feed a disease. Plato describes where that road ends, in the tyrant who can have anything and is the slave of his own cravings, who trusts no one and has no friend.": "c97f8dc2fefe",
"The magistrate owed his career to him.": "b22d4c07d9cb",
"The magistrates half of the argument deserves a hearing.": "32b0f39faf0f",
"The only people who stand to gain.": "4e029ebebbcb",
"The petty man, when alone, stops at nothing.": "533835bd8e87",
"The reply is sound as far as it goes, and it concedes the main point. It says that we should be just because we are always, in effect, watched. It has nothing to say to the person who really does hold the ring. Experiments suggest that such a person is not rare. When people are given a chance to cheat a little without any risk of discovery, a large number do, and they cheat less when a picture of a pair of eyes is hung on the wall.": "5222334d38f8",
"The reply is sound as far as it goes.": "38d3a47c5f00",
"Two claims are packed together here. One is a claim about character. A person is made by what he does repeatedly, and most of what anyone does is done unobserved, so the private hour is where one becomes what one publicly is. The ring would not hide Gyges from the one observer who is always present and whose opinion he must live with. This is Yang Zhens I know, and it is why Confucius says of the gentleman that if he looks within and finds nothing to be ashamed of, he has nothing to fear. The other claim is about knowledge. We are poor at hiding what we are, and a habit of small dishonesty shows in the face. The first claim is close to Platos. Neither Plato nor the Confucians think that the just person is paying a price in private for a public good.": "0ff9de83003d",
